.TH VAIL-CLI 1 "2026-11-03" "vail-cli 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vail-cli \- decode every CW (Morse) signal a sound card can hear
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vail-cli
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vail-cli
decodes all CW signals between 200 and 3900 Hz at once, using the vail engine, and shows what they send. It
listens to the system's default sound input unless told otherwise.
.PP
A
.I file
may be WAV, FLAC or MP3. A file of
.B \-
reads raw samples from standard input.
.PP
On a terminal, a sound device or a stream of samples opens the full-screen view. A recording, a pipe or a file for
standard output gives lines of text instead.
.SH INPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the sound input devices and exit.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " " \fIDEV\fR
Use this device: its number in the list, or any part of its name, in upper or lower case, that matches
only one device.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-channel " " \fIN\fR
Decode only this channel of the input, counting from 1: on stereo, 1 is left and 2 is right. By default the channels
are mixed, so dual-receiver rigs decode both receivers.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-band " " \fILO\-HI\fR
Only decode signals between
.I LO
and
.I HI
Hz.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-seconds " " \fIN\fR
Stop after
.I N
seconds of audio.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-record " " \fIFILE\fR
Also record the audio to a WAV file, at 8 kHz, for replaying later.
.SH RAW SAMPLES
These describe the samples on standard input, with a
.I file
of
.BR \- .
They are always little-endian, and resampled if need be.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-rate " " \fIHZ\fR
Samples per second, from 4000 to 768000. The default is 8000.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-format " " \fIFMT\fR
.B s16
for signed 16-bit, the default, or
.B f32
for 32-bit floating point.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-channels " " \fIN\fR
The number of interleaved channels. The default is 1.
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-view
Show the full-screen view, whatever the source is. It is drawn on the controlling terminal, so standard output
can go to a file or a pipe at the same time. It is an error if there is no terminal to use, or if this copy was built
without ncurses, which
.B \-\-version
says.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-plain
Print finished lines of text only, even on a terminal, with no escape codes. This suits a screen reader. Warnings about
the input level go to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Print finished lines as JSON Lines.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Also show text that does not read like Morse. See
.BR "TEXT THAT IS NOT MORSE" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the versions, and whether this copy has the full-screen view, and exit.
.PP
A value may be written
.BR "\-\-band 500\-900" ,
.B \-\-band=500\-900
or
.BR \-b500\-900 .
Single letters without values may be run together, as in
.BR \-Ta .
.SH FULL-SCREEN VIEW
The top line gives the source, the band, the time and the input level. Below it, each signal has a row with its pitch, the
engine's estimate of its SNR in dB, in the engine's own narrow bandwidth, its speed in WPM, whether it is decoding or has
paused, and its newest text, which can still change. Below those is the log of finished lines, and warnings are at the
bottom.
.TP
.BR q ", " Ctrl+C
Quit.
.TP
.B c
Clear the log. Its lines are still printed when you quit.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down
Scroll the log a line.
.TP
.BR PgUp ", " PgDn
Scroll it a page.
.TP
.BR Home ", " End
Go to the oldest line, or back to following the newest.
.TP
.B ?
Show help.
.PP
If standard output is that terminal, the finished lines are printed when the view closes, up to the last 20000. When the
audio ends by itself, the view stays until you quit.
.PP
.B NO_COLOR
in the environment turns colour off.
.SH OUTPUT
Standard output carries one line for each finished piece of text:
.PP
.RS
.nf
15:33:16   500  CQ CQ CQ DE K1ABC K1ABC K1ABC PSE K
.fi
.RE
.PP
The fields are the time the text started, the pitch in Hz, and the text. The time is UTC for a sound device
and the time from the start for a file or a stream. Words have one space between them, and prosigns are written
as
.BR <SK> .
.PP
A line is printed once its text is final: when the signal has been quiet for 4 seconds, or when 60 characters
can no longer be revised by the decoder. Without the full-screen view, a sound device on a terminal shows signals still
being heard and the input level below the finished lines. They are never written to a pipe or a file.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-json ,
each line is an object:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"time":12.3,"utc":"2026-11-03T15:33:16Z","channel":7,"frequency":500,"snr":12,"wpm":25,"text":"CQ CQ"}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B time
is seconds from the start of the audio.
.B utc
is there only for a sound device. Lines with the same
.B channel
came from one channel of the engine.
.SH TEXT THAT IS NOT MORSE
The decoder reads anything that keys on and off as Morse, and gives no measure of how well it fits. Speech and music
come out as many short pieces of letters such as E and T, so what reads like that is left out, and a note on standard
error says how many lines were. This is judged from the text alone, so:
.IP \(bu 2
RTTY and other data modes, which decode to random-looking text, still print. Real text of random-looking letters, such as
code groups, is why they must.
.IP \(bu 2
A very short real message of nothing but the letters E, T, I, A and N is left out.
.PP
.B \-\-all
shows everything.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including stopping with Ctrl+C or because whatever was reading the output has gone away.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error, such as a missing device, a device that stops, output that cannot be written, or no terminal for
.BR \-\-view .
If a recording cannot be written, that is reported at once and decoding goes on without it.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
vail-cli -l
vail-cli -d "USB Audio"
vail-cli -d 2 -c 1 -b 500-1500 -r session.wav
vail-cli -T >> band.log
vail-cli | grep --line-buffered K1ABC
vail-cli --json | jq -r .text
vail-cli recording.wav > text.log
arecord -t raw -f S16_LE -r 48000 | vail-cli -R 48000 -
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/TheRealKamisama/vail-decoder
